        '<p>A constructor in JavaScript is typically used to initialize the prototypes of objects, set default values, or perform other initialization tasks such as initializing functions.</p><p><br></p><p>In constructor functions, the constructor is named after the object,&nbsp;</p><p>while in classes, the constructor is defined using the "constructor" keyword</p><p><br></p><pre class="ql-syntax" spellcheck="false">function User (name, age) {\n&nbsp;&nbsp;this.name = name;\n&nbsp;&nbsp;this.age = age;\n}\n\nclass Car {\n&nbsp;constructor(brand, price) {&nbsp;// Constructor\n&nbsp;&nbsp;this.carname = brand;\n&nbsp;&nbsp;this.carprice = price;\n&nbsp;}\n}\n</pre><p><br></p><p>In React, the <strong>constructor</strong> is primarily used in class components to:</p><ul><li><strong>To Initialize State</strong></li></ul><p>\t\t\t\tThe constructor allows you to set the initial state of the component (this.state = {}).</p><ul><li><strong>To Bind Event Handlers</strong></li></ul><p>\t\t\t\tIf event handler methods are not arrow functions, they need to be bound in the constructor (this.method = this.method.bind(this);).</p><p><br></p><p>Personally, I prefer to use Functional Component. No "this", no "constructor" needed.</p><p><br></p>',
