      title: '⭐ What is propotype in javascript',
      content:
        '<p>A <strong>prototype</strong> in JavaScript is an <strong>object</strong> that other objects inherit properties and methods from. Every JavaScript function has a "propotype" property, which allows you to <strong>add shared properties and methods</strong> to all instances of an object.</p><p><br></p><pre class="ql-syntax" spellcheck="false">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;// Constructor initialize a Person object\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;function Person(first, last, age, eye) {\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;this.firstName = first;\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;this.lastName = last;\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;this.age = age;\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;this.eyeColor = eye;\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Person.prototype.nationality = "English"; // Thêm mới 1 prototype\n\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;const John = new Person("John", "Doe", 50, "blue");\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;console.log(John.nationality) // English\n</pre><p><br></p><p>When we access a property or method from an object in JavaScript, the JavaScript engine first looks for that property or method directly within the object itself. If it cannot find the property or method within the object, it then searches the prototype of the object\'s constructor.</p><p><br></p><p>For example, let\'s consider a person object. When we attempt to access John.nationality, JavaScript searches for the nationality property within the John object. (the direct properties inside the bracelet "{ }")</p><p>If it doesn\'t find nationality directly within John, it then looks for that in Person constructor\'s prototype (let\'s say, when create a Person with a constructor, we also make all Person object have "propotype.nationality" = English) and found "nationality = English".</p><p><br></p><p>Thus, John\'s nationality is English</p><p><br></p><blockquote><strong><u>Key Points About Prototypes</u></strong></blockquote><p>All JavaScript objects have a prototype</p><p><br></p><ul><li>When you create an object with a constructor function, it automatically gets a prototype property.</li><li>Prototype properties/methods are shared among all instances. This saves memory because instead of copying methods for each object, all instances share them.</li></ul><p><br></p><p>Objects can access properties/methods from their prototype chain</p><ul><li>If a property or method is not found in the object itself, JavaScript looks up the prototype chain.</li></ul>',
