      title: 'What is a Rest Operator',
      content:
        '<h3><strong><u>1. In a function:</u></strong></h3><p>- In a function, the rest operator collects all the remaining parameters of the function and puts them into an array. Now, for example, we have a function:</p><p><br></p><pre class="ql-syntax" spellcheck="false">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;function checkRest(...rest: any) {\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;console.log(rest) // output: [1,2,3,4,5]\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}\n\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;checkRest(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)\n</pre><p><br></p><h3><strong><u>2. In destructuring:</u></strong></h3><p><br></p><p>In destructuring, a rest operator represents all the remaining values of an array or object, excluding the elements that we have already destructured. For example:</p><p><br></p><p>Ví dụ:&nbsp;</p><pre class="ql-syntax" spellcheck="false">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;const [a, b, ...rest] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;console.log(rest); // Output: [3, 4, 5]\n\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;const { x, y, ...props } = { x: 1, y: 2, z: 3, w: 4 };\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;console.log(props); // Output: { z: 3, w: 4 }\n</pre><p><br></p><p>This appear quite common, for example: </p><p><br></p><pre class="ql-syntax" spellcheck="false"> const { firstProp, secondProp, ...otherProps } = props\n</pre><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p>',
